{
 "duration": 57.47,
 "captions": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "text": "One generator can energize miles of power lines.",
   "end": 3.51
  },
  {
   "start": 3.51,
   "text": "If you plug it into your wall, which two kinds of people does the CPSC say are at risk?",
   "end": 9.35
  },
  {
   "start": 9.35,
   "text": "The power flows backward, out of your house.",
   "end": 12.11
  },
  {
   "start": 12.11,
   "text": "The transformer outside steps the voltage up.",
   "end": 15.04
  },
  {
   "start": 15.04,
   "text": "NIOSH says up to seven thousand two hundred volts.",
   "end": 18.53
  },
  {
   "start": 18.53,
   "text": "Down the block, a lineman is fixing the dead line, and it goes live.",
   "end": 22.75
  },
  {
   "start": 22.75,
   "text": "Crews are told to treat every line as live.",
   "end": 25.65
  },
  {
   "start": 25.65,
   "text": "He stops in time.",
   "end": 27.01
  },
  {
   "start": 27.01,
   "text": "But he's not the only one at risk.",
   "end": 29.02
  },
  {
   "start": 29.02,
   "text": "The CPSC says neighbors on the same transformer face an electrocution risk too.",
   "end": 34.4
  },
  {
   "start": 34.4,
   "text": "Here's the fix.",
   "end": 35.45
  },
  {
   "start": 35.45,
   "text": "For your house wiring, have a qualified electrician install a transfer switch.",
   "end": 40.01
  },
  {
   "start": 40.01,
   "text": "Otherwise, plug your appliances straight into the generator.",
   "end": 43.48
  },
  {
   "start": 43.48,
   "text": "The CPSC says use heavy duty cords made for outdoor use.",
   "end": 47.73
  },
  {
   "start": 47.73,
   "text": "Keep it outside, at least twenty feet from doors, windows and vents, the CDC says.",
   "end": 53.4
  },
  {
   "start": 53.4,
   "text": "That way, one generator runs your stuff, not the power lines.",
   "end": 57.47
  }
 ],
